2026-06-30 15:45:51,526 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-99f7f87f-1781-4fbb-86d0-b45f42bdea25', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nEpitope and Paratope Mapping of a SUMO-Remnant Antibody Using Cross-Linking Mass Spectrometry and Molecular Docking\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe small ubiquitin-like modifier (SUMO) is an important post-translational modification that regulates the function of various proteins essential for DNA damage repair, genome integrity, and cell homeostasis. To identify protein SUMOylation effectively, an enrichment step is necessary, often requiring exogenous gene expression in cells and immunoaffinity purification of SUMO-remnant peptides following tryptic digestion. Previously, an antibody was developed to enrich tryptic peptides containing the remnant NQTGG on the receptor lysine, although the specifics of the structural interaction motif remained unclear. This study integrates de novo sequencing, intact mass spectrometry, cross-linking mass spectrometry, and molecular docking to elucidate the structural interaction motifs of a SUMO-remnant antibody. Additional cross-linking experiments were performed using SUMOylated peptides and high-field asymmetric waveform ion mobility spectrometry (FAIMS) to enhance the sensitivity and confirm interactions at the paratope interface. This study establishes a robust framework for characterizing antibody–antigen interactions, offering valuable insights into the structural basis of SUMO-remnant peptide recognition.\n\n[METHODS]\nMaterials and Methods\nDe Novo Sequencing of SUMO-Remnant Antibody\nThe sample preparation protocol was an adaptation of Peng et al. with a few modifications. Briefly, the SUMO-remnant antibody (UMO-1–7–7, available upon request to Abcam) was denatured in 2% sodium deoxycholate (SDC), 200 mM Tris-HCl, and 10 mM Tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP, Thermo Fisher Scientific), pH 8.0, at 95 °C for 10 min, followed by 30 min incubation at 37 °C. The sample was then alkylated with 20 mM 2-chloroacetamide (Aldrich). For selected proteolytic digestion, 3 μg of antibody was digested by one of the following proteases: trypsin (Promega), chymotrypsin (Promega), lys-C (Wako), glu-C (Promega), asp-N (Promega), thermolysin (Promega), and elastase (Promega) in a 1:50 ratio (w/w) in a total volume of 100 μL of 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate at 37 °C for 16 h. For sequential digestion, the antibody was first digested with trypsin for 16 h, followed by the addition of chymotrypsin and incubation for 4 h at 37 °C. For digestion with pepsin (Promega), 3 μg of antibody was digested at 37 °C and pH 2.0 for 16 h. After digestion, SDC was removed by adding 2 μL of formic acid (FA), and the sample was centrifuged at 14,000g for 20 min. The supernatant containing proteolytic peptides was collected and desalted using Ultra Micro Spin columns (The Nest Group) according to the manufacturer’s instructions.\nVacuum-dried peptides were resuspended in 4% FA and analyzed by liquid chromatography–mass spectrometry/MS (LC-MS/MS) using an Easy nLC1000 coupled to an Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Peptide separation was performed on a custom C18 column (Jupiter, Phenomenex, 3 μm, 300 Å, 150 μm × 30 cm) with a flow rate of 600 nL/min and a linear gradient of 5–38% acetonitrile (ACN,0.2% FA) in 105 min, followed by 3 min at 90% ACN (0.2%FA) and 12 min re-equilibration at 5% ACN (0.2% FA). Full MS scans were acquired at a resolution of 60,000 at m/z 200, with a target AGC of 5E5 and a maximum injection time of 120 ms. MS/MS scans were acquired in HCD mode with a stepped collision energy of 25, 35, and 50 at a resolution of 30,000 using a loop count of 20, a target AGC 2E3, and a maximum injection time of 250 ms. The MS/MS triggering threshold was set at 8E3, and the dynamic exclusion of the previously acquired precursor was enabled for 30 s. All raw files were searched with the PEAKS AB engine (Bioinformatics Solutions, Inc., Version 2.0). Parent and fragment mass tolerances were 12 ppm and 0.02 Da, respectively. A maximum of 8 variable modifications were allowed per peptide, including oxidation (MHW), deamidation (NQ), carbamidomethylation (C), and Pyro-glu from Q.\nIntact Mass Analysis\nThe SUMO-remnant antibody was digested with IdeS (FabRICATOR, Genovis), deglycosylated with PNGase F (Thermo Fisher Scientific), and reduced with 5 mM TCEP prior to MS analysis. Intact mass analysis was performed on a Q-Exactive HF BioPharma platform (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Proteins were loaded on a C4 precolumn (Optimize Technologies) and separated on a custom C8 column (Aeris C8, Phenomenex, 3.6 μm, 200 Å, 150 μm × 25 cm) connected to a Nanoflex electrospray source. Proteins were loaded on a C4 precolumn (Optimize Technologies) and eluted at 600 nL/min with a linear gradient of 5–80% solvent B (80% ACN, 0.2% FA) in 40 min and held at 100% B for 5 min. Full MS scans were obtained at a resolution of 240,000, with 3 microscans and a maximum IT of 200 ms with an AGC target of 1e6. Deconvolution was conducted using Thermo Biopharma Finder software version 5.1 with the Xtract algorithm.\nSUMOylation Assay\nIn vitro SUMOylation was performed as previously described. Briefly, recombinant 6xHis-SUMO3-Q87R/Q88N was incubated at 37 °C with the SAE1/SAE2 heterodimer protein, UBC9, and 1 μL of 100 mM MgATP. The sample was reduced with 5 mM TCEP and alkylated with 10 mM 2-chloroacetamide. Digestion was performed overnight with trypsin at a 1:50 enzyme-to-peptide ratio. Peptides were desalted using an Ultra Micro Spin column.\nImmunopurification of SUMO Peptides and Cross-Linking Mass Spectrometry\nDesalted peptides or synthetic peptides containing the NQTGG side chain were incubated overnight at 4 °C with the SUMO-remnant antibody in PBS or immobilized on MagReSyn Protein A magnetic beads. The peptides/antibodies were then cross-linked with either DSS, DSSO, or TBDSPP (Thermo Fisher Scientific) at a final concentration of 2 mM. The cross-linking reaction was carried out for 1 h at room temperature and quenched with 500 mM NH4HCO3 to obtain a final concentration of 20 mM. Samples were then transferred on a 3 K molecular cut-off (VWR), washed twice with 200 μL of 50 mM NH4HCO3, and centrifuged at 13,000g for 10 min. Samples were reconstituted in 190 μL of 50 mM NH4HCO3 reduced with 5 mM TCEP and alkylated with 10 mM 2-chloroacetamide. Digestion was performed overnight with chymotrypsin at a 1:20 enzyme-to-peptide ratio. Peptides were desalted using Ultra Micro Spin columns. TBDSPP-cross-linked peptides were enriched with a Titansphere Phos-TiO kit (GL Science, Tokyo, Japan).\nLC-MS/MS analyses of cross-linked peptides were performed on an Orbitrap Exploris 480. Peptides were loaded on a C4 precolumn (Optimize Technologies) and separated on a C18 column (Jupiter, Phenomenex, 3 μm, 300 Å, 150 μm × 30 cm) with a flow rate of 600 nL/min and a linear gradient of 10–100% solvent B (80% ACN, 0.2% FA) in 100 min and held at 100% B for 2 min. Full MS scans were obtained from m/z 350 to 1100 at a resolution of 240,000, a maximum IT of 50 ms, with a normalized AGC target of 100%. MS/MS spectra of precursor ions with charge states 2–8 were acquired for a maximum of 3 s at a resolution of 45,000, intensity threshold 4000, normalized AGC target at 50%, and a maximum injection time at 1000 ms. The isolation window was set to 1.2 Th with a 34% HCD normalized collision energy (NCE). A dynamic exclusion time of 30 s was used for all of the MS/MS acquisitions. For samples cross-linked with TBDSPP, analyses were also performed on an Orbitrap Exploris 480 using a FAIMS Pro device (Thermo Fisher Scientific) operating at standard resolution mode (inner and outer electrodes maintained at 100 °C) with nitrogen as a carrier gas flowing at 4 L/min. 3-CV combinations (−40/–55/–70) were selected. For samples cross-linked with DSSO, raw files were searched with MaxLynx integrated into the MaxQuant environment with the suggested parameters in the step-by-step guideline with few modifications. Briefly, the second specificity of DSSO was modified as KSTY, and digestion was set as specific with chymotrypsin and 3 missed cleavages. The minimum peptide length was set at 5, and the maximum peptide mass as 10,000 Da. Finally, a PSM FDR cross-link of 5% was used. For sample cross-linked with TBDSPP, data were analyzed using the XlinkX node of Proteome Discoverer 2.3 and the XlinkX_Cleavable processing and consensus workflows, selecting cross-links with a False Discovery Rate of 5%. Finally, cross-linked samples with DSS were searched with pLink 2. Custom parameters were used as follows: nonspecific digestion with up to 5 missed cleavages, 20 ppm for precursor and fragment tolerance, fixed carbamidomethylation [C], variable oxidation [M], and deamidation [NQ] with 5% FDR.\nEThcD\nEThcD analysis was performed on an Orbitrap Ascend with an Evosep One system. Peptides were loaded onto individual Evosep for desalting, washed with 20 μL of 0.1% FA, and then separated with a 15 cm × 75 μm IonOpticks Aurora Series UHPLC column and a Whisper Zoom 20 SPD gradient. Full MS scans were obtained from m/z 400 to 800 at a resolution of 120,000, a maximum IT of 50 ms, with a normalized AGC target of 100%. DDA MS/MS spectra of precursor ions with charge states 2–6 were acquired for a maximum of 3 s at a resolution of 60,000, a scan range of 140–2000 m/z, normal AGC target at 1000%, isolation window set to 1 Th, ETD reaction time of 123 ms, and HCD supplemental activation collisional energy set at 60%. A dynamic exclusion time of 60 s was used for all MS/MS acquisitions.\nTargeted LC-MS/MS Analyses Using Parallel Reaction Monitoring\nParallel reaction monitoring (PRM) was performed on a Q-Exactive HF BioPharma platform with an Easy-nLC 1200. Peptides were loaded on a C4 precolumn (Optimize Technologies) and separated on a homemade C18 column (JupiterC18, Phenomenex, 3 μm, 300 Å, 150 μm × 30 cm) with a flow rate of 600 nL/min and a linear gradient of 6–45% solvent B (80% ACN, 0.2% FA) in 105 min and held at 90% B for 3 min. HCD at 27% with a 1.4 Th isolation window was used for the PRM experiment. MS/MS resolution, automatic gain control, and injection time were set at 60 K, 5E4, and 150 ms, respectively. The MS raw data have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium () via the PRIDE partner repository with the data set identifier PXD055077, PXD055085 and PXD060724.\nStructure Prediction and Molecular Docking\nThe structure of the light chain and the SUMO-remnant peptide was predicted using the ESMFold algorithm, while the heavy chain was predicted using the ColabFold algorithm as implemented on the Colab server. DisVis webserveur− was used to evaluate the information content of MS cross-links and identify possible false positives. The SUMO peptide was then docked using HADDOCK 2.4 using the cross-link distance restraints. The results of the molecular docking were visualized using PyMOL (1.1r1).\nGlycan Search\nA glycopeptide database search was performed on all raw files from de novo sequencing using the glycan search workflow in PEAKS studio 11 against the constructed sequence of the SUMO-remnant antibody. The search parameters were set as follows: up to 3 missed cleavages, 20 ppm precursor mass tolerance, and 0.02 Da fragment mass tolerance; carbamidomethylation (C), deamidation (NQ), oxidation (M), and pyro-glu form Q were set as variable modifications. NH4 adduct was considered with a maximum of one adduct per peptide and a maximum of 2 fucoses.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResult and Discussion\nDe Novo Sequencing of SUMO-Remnant Antibody\nTo identify the paratope of anti-K-NQTGG, we first conducted de novo sequencing and peptide assembly using Peaks Ab software (Figure 1, top panel). Our protocol was adapted from Peng et al. with a few modifications. Specifically, we either used eight proteases with complementary specificities or combined chymotrypsin with trypsin in sequential digestion (Supporting Figures 1 and 2).\nPrevious studies have shown that consecutive proteolytic digestion increases the number of identified proteins, whereas sequential digestion with trypsin and elastase allows for the identification of cross-links in inaccessible regions, as exemplified for the TAF4–12 complex. Conversely, sequential digestion of SUMO-remnant antibody with trypsin and chymotrypsin resulted in fewer peptides (Figures S1 and S2) but provided more fragment ions covering the N-terminus of the light chain, leading to a higher confidence score (indicated by the plot line in Figure S1).\nBefore digestion, the antibody was denatured with sodium deoxycholate at a high temperature in the presence of TCEP. This resulted in nearly complete desulfurization of all cysteine residues in the constructed sequence. Although this conversion has been demonstrated before at lower levels, it should be considered during peptide assembly to avoid incorrect assignment. To mitigate this potential issue, it is advisable to use another reducing reagent, such as DTT, as previously mentioned. In the case of the SUMO-remnant antibody, all cysteine residues in the constructed light and heavy chains were assigned as alanine, except for the one at the C-terminus of the light chain. Under harsher conditions, converting cysteine to alanine yielded more favorable results, considering the desulfurization process. Without denatured conditions, the constructed sequences had more errors, particularly in the third CDR of the light chain, which was poorly covered (results not shown).\nUsing the adapted protocol, we achieved 100% coverage on both light and heavy chains with a score of 99.2% (Supporting Figure 3). Excluding cysteine desulfurization, only two amino acids on the heavy chain needed further confirmation. On the heavy chain, asparagine residues 166 and 378 were incorrectly identified as aspartic acid due to deamidation, which can occur during manufacturing, storage, or sample preparation. Our protein modification profiles revealed a high occurrence of deamidation in tryptic digest (high pH) and a low occurrence in pepsin digest (low pH), consistent with published data on endopeptidase Glu-C at low pH. To differentiate between the isomeric residues leucine and isoleucine, Peaks Ab employed a basic local alignment search tool (BLAST) using the constructed sequence. Additionally, it calculates the number of peptide-spectrum matches (PSMs) from chymotrypsin and pepsin digests, where Ile or Leu residues occur at the C-terminus. These enzymes preferentially cleave at the C-terminus of leucine over isoleucine, aiding in residue-specific assignment. To further improve the confidence in Ile/Leu differentiation, we applied an EThcD approach to both tryptic and chymotryptic digests. Briefly, z-ions were generated through electron-transfer dissociation (ETD) fragmentation of doubly or triply charged peptide precursor ions. These z-ions were subsequently fragmented using higher-energy collisional dissociation (HCD) with broadband ion isolation, producing w-ions that provided diagnostic fragment ions for the differentiation of leucine and isoleucine residues. This approach effectively leveraged the diagnostic w- and z-ions to support accurate residue identification, with results confirmed independently through cDNA sequencing. Representative examples, including tryptic peptides from the complementarity-determining regions (CDRs) of the light and heavy chains, are provided in Supporting Figure 4. Following de novo sequencing of the SUMO-remnant antibody, we next performed a structure prediction for both the light and heavy chains using the AlphaFold and ESMFold.\nA glycan analysis revealed that asparagine 291 (highlighted in purple in Supporting Figure 5) on the heavy chain is modified with over 50 different N-linked glycans with a significant presence of highly fucosylated glycans. Supporting Figure 6 presents a pie chart displaying the distribution of these glycans. The three most prominent glycopeptide spectra, along with their corresponding compositions and highlighted fragments, are shown in Supporting Figure 7. Altogether, the combination of a limited set of enzymes (trypsin, chymotrypsin, asp-N, and trypsin + chymotrypsin) provided good coverage of both the light and heavy chains, validated the sequence predicted from the hybridoma cDNA, and identified N-linked glycans that could not be inferred from cDNA sequencing.\nSequence Validation by Intact Mass Measurements\nNext, we verified the assembled sequence through intact mass analysis (Figure 1, second panel). Briefly, the subunits of the SUMO-remnant antibody were obtained by deglycosylation with PNGase F, digestion with IdeS (FabRICATOR), and reduction with TCEP. Using FabRICATOR (IdeS) allowed us to apply identical resolution settings for both the light and heavy chains, resulting in isotopically resolved spectra. This protease cleaves the antibody just below the hinge, precisely at a single amino acid position (Figure 1, second panel). Upon reduction, it produces the Fc/2 and Fd′ fragments, both of which exhibit a mass similar to that of the light chain. We confirmed one deamidation of the heavy chain in the Fc2 fragment (Figure 2).\nAdditionally, we validated the expected conversion of the N-terminal glutamine to pyro-glutamic acid, resulting in a mass difference of 17 Da in both the light and heavy sequences. Accordingly, we observed a 6 Da mass shift for the light chain and 4 Da for both fragments of the heavy chain (Figure 2 and Supporting Figure 8). We hypothesize that the observed mass difference could be explained by the conservation of intrachain disulfide bonds, which are more solvent-exposed, contrasting with the interchain disulfide bonds. We validated this hypothesis by performing intact mass analyses with and without alkylation. The mass difference between the two monoisotopic masses highlights the cysteine modification and the number of expected S–S bonds. Theoretically, for the reduced/alkylated light chain, we should observe a mass increase of 399 Da corresponding to seven carbamidomethylated cysteines. However, we identified a mass difference of 57 Da, which can be attributed solely to only one carbamidomethylated cysteine, as shown in Supporting Figure 9.\nMoreover, with the constructed sequence for the Fc/2 fragment, we observed an approximate mass shift of 30 Da. This mass difference could be explained by the substitution of alanine 303 to threonine, confirmed by MS/MS with a high-confidence score and validated by intact mass measurements (Supporting Figure 8). This permutation appears to be lot-dependent and does not affect the isolation of the SUMO-remnant antibody (data not shown). Altogether, intact mass measurements from the antibody fragment chains enabled the correlation of the sequence information available from the multienzyme digestion experiments. For convenience, the amino acid sequence of the light and heavy chains of the SUMO-remnant antibody is shown in Supporting Figure 5.\nParatope Mapping by Cross-Linking Mass Spectrometry and Molecular Docking\nFollowing de novo sequencing and validation of the antibody sequence through intact mass measurements, we investigated the interaction occurring at the paratope–epitope interface using cross-linking mass spectrometry (Figure 1, panel 2 bottom panel). Prior to cross-linking with DSS, the SUMO-remnant antibody was incubated with peptide K*NQTGG, where * indicates the isopeptide bound between the epsilon NH2 of Lys and the C-terminus of Gly. To achieve optimal coverage, we employed the same protocol used for de novo sequencing, excluding Lys-C, and used sodium deoxycholate as a denaturing agent. The raw data were analyzed using pLink with nonspecific digestion parameters. Applying a 5% false discovery rate (FDR), we identified 19 cross-linked peptides, including four mapped within the CDR as confirmed by IMGT/DomainGapAlign and ProABC-2 predictions. We next evaluated distance restraints between the SUMO-remnant antibody and the K*NQTGG peptide using the DisVis web server (Supporting Table S1). Each restraint was assigned a Z-score to estimate the likelihood of false positives. DisVis identified solutions consistent with up to 11 restraints, indicating the potential presence of up to eight false positives. To model the interactions between the antibody and the SUMO-remnant pentapeptide, we utilized HADDOCK. Filtered cross-links were incorporated as unambiguous distance restraints, supplemented by center-of-mass restraints, to generate a refined structural model. The top-ranked cluster is shown in Supporting Figure 10. This initial model places the pentapeptide near the C-terminal region of the heavy chain without forming direct interactions with the predicted CDRs. To evaluate the validity of the docking model, we re-examined the filtered restraints excluded during docking and identified them as potential false positives by DisVis. Specifically, we focused on four cross-links mapped to residues within the CDRs. Given the high false discovery rate associated with cross-linking of serine, threonine, and tyrosine residues, we performed validation using parallel reaction monitoring (PRM) analysis on a Q-Exactive mass spectrometer (Supporting Figure 11). This analysis confirmed the precise assignment of all four cross-linked peptides, each located within the 30 Å threshold and proximal to the CDRs, effectively invalidating the initial model. We hypothesize that over-cross-linking may have introduced nonspecific interactions, misinterpreted as true structural information. Consequently, we refined the docking restraints based on the updated DisVis analysis (Figure 3). In the revised model, the pentapeptide occupies a gap between the light and heavy chains, positioned closer to the CDRs, aligning with the validated cross-linking data. This improved model provides a more accurate depiction of the interaction between the antibody and the SUMO-remnant pentapeptide.\nTo further investigate the specificity of the SUMO-remnant antibody and our model, we incubated a pool of synthetic peptides at 50 nM from 40 known SUMO substrates, all containing the NQTGG side chain lysine. Cross-linking was performed in solution using DSS and the raw files were analyzed using MaxLynx integrated into the MaxQuant environment. Chymotrypsin was selected as the proteolytic enzyme due to its comprehensive coverage across both light and heavy chains, whereas trypsin yields fewer peptides near the third CDR of the light chain (Supporting Figures 1 and 2). To address the challenge of the expansion of the computational search space exacerbated by the SUMO remnant, lysine-containing side chain NQTGG was introduced as a linear segment into the peptide FASTA sequence. One drawback of this modification is the reduced sequence database search score due to the number of unidentified fragment ions. Consequently, we opted for a higher FDR and manually validated the MS/MS, specifically scrutinizing the diagnostic SUMO peaks. Using this approach, we identified nine cross-linked peptides containing the NQTGG motif. Among these, one overlapped with previous experiments on peptide K*NQTGG (Supporting Figure 11), six were located within the CDR of the light chain, and three were within the constant region of the heavy chain of the antibody (Supporting Table S2), but identified as potential positive restraint. Furthermore, we conducted additional cross-linking experiments using the peptide FKQEPEPEDELPEAPK, modified with either the SUMO-remnant NQTGG or its reverse sequence, GGTQN (Supporting Figure 12). These experiments confirmed that the SUMO-remnant antibody selectively binds to the NQTGG epitope, while its reverse sequence, GGTQN, is not recognized by the antibody, underscoring the epitope’s orientation specificity.\nTo minimize nonspecific cross-linking and false positives, we next incubated peptide FK*QEPEPEDELPEAPK (*designates modified lysine residues) with the SUMO-remnant antibody previously cross-linked on MagReSyn Protein A magnetic beads. We performed two washes to remove weak and nonspecific interactions prior to cross-linking with DSSO. As in the previous analysis, chymotrypsin was used. Interestingly, our observations revealed only cross-linked peptides containing the NQTGG motif between the first and second CDR of the light chain, which are in close proximity. An example is shown in Figure 4 for the cross-linked peptide YQHRPGQPPKQLIY. Notably, we observed the presence of fragment ions from the SUMO-remnant chain (highlighted in yellow).\nFinally, we performed a cross-linking experiment with a poly SUMO chain protein obtained from in vitro SUMOylation of SUMO3m (Supporting Figure 13), which contains a lysine at position 22. To address sensitivity issues related to the low abundance of the cross-linked peptides, we used TBDSPP for enrichment and gas-phase separation using high-field asymmetric waveform ion mobility spectrometry (FAIMS). This additional separation and enrichment enabled the identification of the tryptic peptide EGVKTENDHINLK derived from SUMO3m, featuring SUMOylated K22 cross-linked with the chymotryptic peptide SLSRY from the heavy chain of the antibody, associated with the first CDR (Supporting Figure 14). These findings align with our previous observations and emphasize the critical interactions at the paratope. Additionally, the results corroborate the predictions illustrated in Figure 5, which were generated using validated restraints (light-K30, light-K46, light-S57, heavy-S27, heavy-Y31, and heavy-Y98), as detailed in Figure 4, and Supporting Figures 9 and 12, with an upper distance limit of 30 Å. The top three HADDOCK models suggest that the NQTGG motif is positioned within a groove formed by the interplay of various complementarity-determining regions (CDRs). This motif establishes multiple contacts with residues in the third CDR of the heavy chain, a region centrally located within the antigen-binding site of both the heavy and light chains. Notably, consistent polar interactions were observed between tyrosine 98, arginine 104, and, to a lesser extent, arginine 95 and serine 99 on the H3 loop, strongly indicating that these residues play a pivotal role in antigen binding.\nWhile we acknowledge the limitations inherent in using cross-linking data and molecular docking to predict antibody–antigen complexes, as highlighted in previous studies, these challenges are further compounded by the absence of models that explicitly account for the branched structure of the SUMO peptide. Nonetheless, our findings are consistent with prior studies that underscore the importance of tyrosine residues in antigen–antibody interactions and highlight the significance of the third CDR in antigen binding. Ultimately, the precise identification of critical residues for binding would require additional experiments, such as the systematic substitution of amino acid residues (alanine scanning), to assess their impact on binding affinity.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
